Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 23:55:03 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 193/209] feat(area): gravity volume function component

The component form of CGravityVolumeObject, minus the 1378 lines of duplicated
spline code it carried. Category "Area", label "Gravity Volume", Singleton.

It binds BY RULE, not by a reference field: the volume is the spline shape on
the same entity, found with GetComponent<IShapeComponent>() and GetSpline(),
with a latched warning naming the entity when there is none. That is the pattern
CAreaComponent::EType::Spline already ships (AreaComponent.cpp:132-151).

Physics is built directly - IPhysicalWorld::AddArea(points, n, radius, pos, rot,
scale) plus pe_params_area - rather than through
IEntityAreaComponent::SetGravityVolume (decision 04, option G2). Two reasons,
both from research/08 section 4. SetGravityVolume only STORES
(AreaProxy.cpp:713-727) and the physics is created in OnEnable(), which nothing
calls when the shape changes, so a legacy gravity volume keeps its old tube
until a visibility toggle or a level reload. And Falloff and Damping are never
serialized (AreaProxy.cpp:516-527), which silently discards authored values.
Here all six parameters are reflected members and the tube is rebuilt on every
shape change.

Details worth recording:

- the points go in as a SCRATCH copy on the stack. AddArea takes a non-const
  Vec3* and the entity-side path it mirrors rewrites its input in place
  (PhysicsProxy.cpp:1591-1593); handing it the shape's own storage would let the
  spline drift further with every rebuild;
- they are converted shape-local -> world -> the unscaled entity space that
  AddArea's pos/rot/scale arguments define, so a rotated or uniformly scaled
  entity is correct;
- a closed spline appends its first point, which CAreaComponent also does -
  except that it has to GUESS closedness from an AABB-centre containment test
  (:139, wrong for a C-shaped contour) because a legacy area shape has no closed
  flag. This one has;
- rebuilds are debounced by the shape itself: BeginEdit/EndEdit coalesce a drag
  into one OnShapeChanged. A Transform reason is ignored entirely, because the
  area is assigned to the entity and the entity moves it, and AddArea is not
  incremental;
- the previewer draws rings around the real curve, so what is drawn and what is
  physicalized are the same shape. In legacy they are not: the display uses a
  quadratic interpolator (GravityVolumeObject.cpp:594-606) while the physics
  uses the raw control points.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
